<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Semna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Semna was a group of Egyptian fortresses at the southern end of the Second Cataract of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> of the Twelfth Dynasty fixed the frontier there in the 19th century BCE with boundary stelae forbidding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> to pass north except to trade. The forts and their temples were flooded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a> after the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a>, though the temples were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/semna.jpg" alt="Semna" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MONNIER Franck (monnierfranck@hotmail.com) · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Semna-vue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Semna lies about 15 miles south of Wadi Halfa, where rocks cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and narrow its flow at the Semna Cataract. The Semna gorge was the narrowest part of the Nile valley and the southern edge of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, in the Batn-El-Hajar, the Belly of the Rock, a land of bare granite ridges and wind-blown sand with only a thin layer of fertile soil. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaohs</a> of the Twelfth Dynasty built a cluster of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> fortresses here: Semna West, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumma">Kumma</a> or Semna East across the river, Semna South about a kilometre upstream, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uronarti">Uronarti</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The forts were among 17 Middle Kingdom strongholds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> that guarded the river and the flow of southern goods into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, pelts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrich-feather">ostrich feathers</a> and enslaved people. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> walls were reinforced with logs, which made them more vulnerable to fire, and they had doubly fortified gates and a protected corridor down to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. A great wall found in the 1960s joined Semna South to Semna West, a sign of the pressure from the south recorded in the Semna Stela and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna-dispatches">Semna Dispatches</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, when the region had become part of an Egyptian empire, Semna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumma">Kumma</a> held temples, houses and cemeteries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>George Reisner investigated the forts in 1924 and 1928. Semna South was excavated by Jean Vercoutter and Sayed Thabit Hassan Thabit for the Sudan Antiquities Service in 1956 and 1957, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a>'s Oriental Institute under Louis Vico Žabkar from 1966 to 1968, before the flooding. Seal impressions in a Twelfth Dynasty dump gave the fort's Egyptian name, Subduer of the Setiu-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a>. A cemetery of about 560 graves held more than 800 people of the Meroitic, X-Group and Christian periods, and a small church probably dates from the ninth to the early eleventh century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a> has covered the forts since 1971.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Semna (Nubia)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Senusret I campaigned as far as the Second Cataract, and Senusret III led several expeditions south, fixing the frontier at <b>Semna</b> with boundary stelae that forbade Nubians to pass northwards except to trade, and clearing a canal through the First Cataract for his boats.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Military</a></li></ul></section>
